1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,600
Send a message from one iPhone to another,
and it shows up in a blue bubble.

2
00:00:05,195 --> 00:00:10,195
Send the same message from an iPhone to
an Android phone, and the bubble is green.

3
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,760
Same words. Same app. Different colour.

4
00:00:14,497 --> 00:00:17,177
For years, that colour came
with real problems.

5
00:00:17,797 --> 00:00:20,537
Blurry videos. Broken group chats.

6
00:00:21,138 --> 00:00:23,958
In 2024, the iPhone finally learned

7
00:00:24,188 --> 00:00:27,838
the same modern messaging standard
Android phones use.

8
00:00:28,209 --> 00:00:30,049
And the bubbles are still green.

9
00:00:30,822 --> 00:00:32,022
So why?

10
00:00:34,147 --> 00:00:36,047
Here's the first surprise.

11
00:00:36,687 --> 00:00:39,467
Green wasn't invented
to mark Android users.

12
00:00:40,218 --> 00:00:45,478
When the iPhone launched in 2007,
every text you sent on it was green.

13
00:00:46,138 --> 00:00:49,598
Every single one.
Even between two iPhones.

14
00:00:50,545 --> 00:00:55,145
Blue only arrived in 2011,
with a new service called iMessage.

15
00:00:55,647 --> 00:00:59,767
So green isn't the odd colour out.
Green is the original.

16
00:01:00,651 --> 00:01:02,171
Blue is the exception.

17
00:01:03,115 --> 00:01:06,255
And the colour doesn't describe
the phone on the other end.

18
00:01:06,681 --> 00:01:09,261
It describes the road
the message travelled.

19
00:01:10,598 --> 00:01:14,038
To see why that matters,
we need to look at those two roads.

20
00:01:14,811 --> 00:01:17,211
The old road is the mobile network.

21
00:01:18,043 --> 00:01:21,403
SMS for text, MMS for pictures and video.

22
00:01:22,065 --> 00:01:24,065
Practically every phone speaks them,

23
00:01:24,145 --> 00:01:27,885
because they belong to the mobile network,
not to the phone makers.

24
00:01:28,467 --> 00:01:29,967
But it's an old road.

25
00:01:30,527 --> 00:01:33,207
An SMS holds a hundred
and sixty characters.

26
00:01:33,769 --> 00:01:38,029
And an MMS video is squeezed
until it fits the operator's size limit,

27
00:01:38,109 --> 00:01:40,409
often about a megabyte, or less.

28
00:01:41,519 --> 00:01:43,379
The new road is the internet.

29
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:49,120
iMessage sends your message as ordinary
data, through Apple's own servers.

30
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:51,640
No operator in the middle.

31
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:54,660
No size limits from the flip-phone era.

32
00:01:55,355 --> 00:01:57,555
Read receipts. Typing indicators.

33
00:01:57,785 --> 00:01:59,955
Full-quality photos.

34
00:02:00,335 --> 00:02:02,355
Group chats that actually work.

35
00:02:02,886 --> 00:02:05,846
But Apple's servers
only talk to Apple devices.

36
00:02:06,373 --> 00:02:08,073
So when an iPhone sent a message

37
00:02:08,303 --> 00:02:11,433
to anything else,
it couldn't take the new road.

38
00:02:11,737 --> 00:02:13,697
It fell back to the old one.

39
00:02:14,350 --> 00:02:16,390
Blue means Apple carried it.

40
00:02:16,806 --> 00:02:18,786
Green means someone else did.

41
00:02:20,105 --> 00:02:22,185
That's the whole technical answer.

42
00:02:22,825 --> 00:02:25,405
And it would be a boring story,
if it ended there.

43
00:02:26,005 --> 00:02:27,305
It doesn't.

44
00:02:28,901 --> 00:02:32,541
Because falling back to the old road
made the conversation worse.

45
00:02:33,199 --> 00:02:35,199
Not just for the Android user.

46
00:02:35,499 --> 00:02:37,199
For everyone in the chat.

47
00:02:37,927 --> 00:02:40,347
Add one Android phone
to a group of iPhones,

48
00:02:40,807 --> 00:02:42,907
and the whole group dropped to MMS.

49
00:02:43,655 --> 00:02:45,695
Videos arrived as a smudge.

50
00:02:45,815 --> 00:02:49,115
Reactions arrived as text.
"Liked an image."

51
00:02:49,676 --> 00:02:51,896
And for the person
everyone was waiting on,

52
00:02:51,976 --> 00:02:54,076
it looked like their phone
was the problem.

53
00:02:56,436 --> 00:02:59,556
In the United States,
this became a social thing.

54
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,640
In 2022, the Wall Street Journal

55
00:03:02,870 --> 00:03:06,280
reported that American teenagers
dreaded the green bubble.

56
00:03:06,911 --> 00:03:10,811
Some said they'd been singled out,
even ostracised, for switching.

57
00:03:11,593 --> 00:03:14,493
The same year,
a survey of American teens found

58
00:03:14,723 --> 00:03:17,233
that eighty-seven per cent
owned an iPhone.

59
00:03:17,783 --> 00:03:22,843
And in 2024, when the US government
sued Apple for monopolising smartphones,

60
00:03:23,437 --> 00:03:25,537
the green bubble was in the complaint.

61
00:03:26,128 --> 00:03:27,908
So this wasn't a niche complaint.

62
00:03:28,508 --> 00:03:30,308
In America, the colour mattered.

63
00:03:32,988 --> 00:03:34,788
But here's the strange part.

64
00:03:35,648 --> 00:03:38,148
In most of the world,
it barely registered.

65
00:03:38,965 --> 00:03:41,345
Ask someone in Germany, Brazil or India

66
00:03:41,575 --> 00:03:44,725
about green bubbles,
and you may get a blank look.

67
00:03:45,169 --> 00:03:49,009
And a big part of the reason
goes back to the price of a text message.

68
00:03:49,681 --> 00:03:53,321
In much of Europe,
texts often cost money, one by one.

69
00:03:53,595 --> 00:03:58,595
So when smartphones arrived, people jumped
to apps that sent messages over data.

70
00:03:59,323 --> 00:04:00,563
Above all, WhatsApp.

71
00:04:01,519 --> 00:04:04,799
And WhatsApp looks the same
on an iPhone and on Android.

72
00:04:05,455 --> 00:04:08,095
No blue. No green. No fallback.

73
00:04:09,351 --> 00:04:13,391
In the United States, texting had
become cheap, and often unlimited.

74
00:04:13,883 --> 00:04:17,223
So there was little reason
to leave the phone's built-in app.

75
00:04:17,819 --> 00:04:21,999
Americans kept texting, and more
and more of them did it on an iPhone.

76
00:04:22,822 --> 00:04:27,482
The green bubble problem took hold where
texting was cheap and iPhones were common.

77
00:04:30,502 --> 00:04:33,602
So why didn't Apple just
put iMessage on Android?

78
00:04:34,310 --> 00:04:37,090
Inside Apple, people did suggest it.

79
00:04:37,670 --> 00:04:43,430
Emails that came out in a court case show
executives discussing the idea in 2013.

80
00:04:43,945 --> 00:04:46,585
But one executive argued
that iMessage on Android

81
00:04:46,815 --> 00:04:51,925
would make it easier for iPhone families
to give their kids Android phones.

82
00:04:52,659 --> 00:04:54,879
iMessage wasn't just a feature.

83
00:04:55,459 --> 00:04:56,999
It was a reason to stay.

84
00:04:57,564 --> 00:05:01,724
And in 2022, when someone told Apple's CEO
he couldn't send

85
00:05:01,954 --> 00:05:05,644
his mother certain videos,
because she had an Android phone,

86
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:08,480
the answer was: "Buy your mom an iPhone."

87
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:14,140
But Apple isn't the only reason
this took so long.

88
00:05:14,736 --> 00:05:17,676
Android had no single answer to iMessage.

89
00:05:18,437 --> 00:05:21,157
Google launched one messaging app
after another.

90
00:05:21,617 --> 00:05:23,477
Talk. Hangouts. Allo.

91
00:05:24,174 --> 00:05:26,574
And retired them, one after another.

92
00:05:27,614 --> 00:05:31,374
The real successor to SMS
was supposed to be RCS.

93
00:05:32,134 --> 00:05:33,914
Rich Communication Services.

94
00:05:34,795 --> 00:05:39,415
A standard designed by the mobile industry
in the late 2000s.

95
00:05:39,619 --> 00:05:44,779
But RCS depended on operators to switch
it on, and for years, many didn't.

96
00:05:45,304 --> 00:05:48,304
In the end, Google ran much of it
on its own servers.

97
00:05:50,864 --> 00:05:54,804
And then, in November 2023,
Apple made an announcement.

98
00:05:55,696 --> 00:05:58,156
The iPhone would support RCS.

99
00:05:58,843 --> 00:06:00,903
Apple didn't say why now.

100
00:06:01,541 --> 00:06:05,281
The timing lined up with
a European competition investigation.

101
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:11,040
Others pointed to China, where new
phones were being pushed to support RCS.

102
00:06:11,867 --> 00:06:14,407
A few weeks later,
a small app called Beeper Mini

103
00:06:14,637 --> 00:06:16,787
let Android phones send real iMessages.

104
00:06:18,235 --> 00:06:20,175
Apple shut it out within days.

105
00:06:22,515 --> 00:06:27,155
Blue stayed Apple's.
But in 2024, RCS arrived.

106
00:06:27,903 --> 00:06:30,543
iPhones and Android phones
could finally share

107
00:06:30,773 --> 00:06:34,403
read receipts, typing indicators,
and proper photos.

108
00:06:35,103 --> 00:06:38,603
In 2026, Apple and Google
began rolling out encryption

109
00:06:38,833 --> 00:06:43,703
for those messages, first as a beta,
and only with some operators.

110
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:47,170
So the road between the two
is finally paved.

111
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:51,820
Which brings us back to the question.

112
00:06:52,651 --> 00:06:54,591
Why is the bubble still green?

113
00:06:55,971 --> 00:06:58,471
Because RCS is not iMessage.

114
00:06:59,267 --> 00:07:02,427
It's a newer, better road,
but it isn't Apple's road.

115
00:07:03,117 --> 00:07:06,357
Blue is reserved
for messages Apple carries itself.

116
00:07:06,853 --> 00:07:09,753
So the colour never really meant
"this phone is worse".

117
00:07:10,469 --> 00:07:12,809
It meant "this message isn't ours".

118
00:07:15,679 --> 00:07:18,599
And that's the quiet change
behind the whole story.

119
00:07:19,231 --> 00:07:23,311
SMS belonged to the operators,
and so it worked with everyone.

120
00:07:23,978 --> 00:07:26,658
The messengers that replaced it
belong to the companies

121
00:07:26,888 --> 00:07:29,018
that make the phones and the apps.

122
00:07:29,485 --> 00:07:33,901
And a company that owns the road
gets to decide who's allowed to use it.

123
00:07:33,981 --> 00:07:35,941
And what colour you are when you do.

124
00:07:37,681 --> 00:07:39,541
So here's a question for you.

125
00:07:40,221 --> 00:07:42,141
If every bubble turned
the same colour tomorrow,

126
00:07:42,371 --> 00:07:44,421
would anyone actually switch phones?

127
00:07:44,901 --> 00:07:46,451
Let me know in the comments.
